     1	"""Transcript readers: turn an agent's session log into one normalized event stream.
     2	
     3	``AttachBridge`` is agent-agnostic — it does Telegram I/O (progress messages, the live tool
     4	status bubble, the typing indicator) the same way for every agent. Each reader here knows the
     5	on-disk transcript format of one agent and maps its records to a common set of events:
     6	
     7	  ``turn_start`` — a new turn began. Codex writes ``task_started``; Claude Code has no such
     8	                   record, so for it the bridge starts the turn from the inbound message.
     9	  ``user``       — a user message (``Ev.text``). Used to detect whether the turn came from
    10	                   Telegram (origin prefix) so only those turns are forwarded.
    11	  ``text``       — assistant text to forward as a kept progress/final message. ``Ev.key`` is a
    12	                   stable dedup id; ``Ev.final`` hints this is the final answer.
    13	  ``tool``       — a tool/command call, summarized for the one-line status bubble. ``Ev.key`` is
    14	                   the call id (so the same call isn't pushed twice).
    15	  ``turn_end``   — the turn finished. Codex writes ``task_complete`` (so no Stop hook is needed!);
    16	                   Claude Code has none, so the bridge ends the turn via its Stop-hook marker / idle.
    17	
    18	Why an abstraction instead of an ``if agent == ...`` in the bridge: the two formats differ a lot
    19	(Claude = one assistant record with text+tool_use blocks and a uuid; Codex = separate event_msg /
    20	response_item lines, no uuid, but an explicit task_complete). Keeping that knowledge in small
    21	readers makes the bridge identical for both and easy to extend to a third agent later.
